1 OCCU graaf van Benthem, Graaf van Rheineck, in het jaar 1140 ook even paltsgraaf aan de Rijn, Pzalzgreve vid Rhen, Comte palatin, du Rhin, Count of Salm, Count of Bentheim, Count of Rhein
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} '''Otto I, Count of Salm'''
2 CONT
2 CONT Otto I, Count of Salm (c. 1080 – 1150) was a German nobleman. He was a ruling count of Salm and from 1125 to 1137, he was co-ruler of the County Palatine of the Rhine with his stepson William.
2 CONT
2 CONT His parents were the German anti-king Hermann of Salm and his wife Sophia of Formbach. Around 1115, he married Gertrude of Northeim, the widow of Count Palatine Sigfried of the Rhine and heiress of Margrave Henry of Frisia and Nor
2 CONC theim and Gertrude of Brunswick, who was the sister of Richenza of Northeim, the consort of Emperor Lothair III.
2 CONT
2 CONT He constructed Rheineck Castle and from c. 1124, he styled himself Otto of Rheineck, after his castle. 
2 CONT {{https://www.geni.com/media/proxy?media_id=6000000221701301125&size=large}}
2 CONT ''Burg Rheineck Sammlung Duncker between 1857 and 1883 by Theodor Albert (Magdeburg 1822-1867, Berlin), Alexander Duncker (1813-1897) courtesy Wikimedia Commons''
2 CONT
2 CONT After his stepson Count Palatine William of the Rhine had died in 1140, he claimed the County Palatine of the Rhine. However, King Conrad III of Germany, who had been elected in 1138, decided that the County Palatine was a complet
2 CONC ed fief, and hence fell back to the King, who gave it to his brother-in-law and devoted supporter Herman of Stahleck.
2 CONT
2 CONT Otto managed to keep Treis Castle until 1148, and Rheineck Castle until 1151. In 1148, war broke out between Otto and the Emperor. The Emperor took Treis Castle in 1148, and gave it to the Elector of Trier. In 1151, he captured an
2 CONC d destroyed Rheineck Castle.
2 CONT
2 CONT Family
2 CONT Otto married Gertrude, daughter of Henry, Margrave of Frisia.[1] They had several children, including:
2 CONT
2 CONT Otto II (c. 1115 – 1148/1149), fought against Hermann von Stahleck to recapture the County Palatine of the Rhine and was taken prisoner in 1148. He was later strangled at Schönburg Castle, near Oberwesel in 1148 or 1149
2 CONT Sophia, married Dirk VI, Count of Holland (d. 6 August 1157)
2 CONT Beatrix, married Wilbrand I, Margrave of Frisia
2 CONT
2 CONT References
2 CONT Johann Christian von Stramberg: Rheinischer Antiquarius, part III, vol. 5, p. 490 ff
2 CONT M. Schaab, Geschichte der Kurpfalz (Kohlhammer Verlag 1988)
2 CONT
2 CONT Source: Wikipedia contributors. "Otto I, Count of Salm." Wikipedia, The Free Encyclopedia. Wikipedia, The Free Encyclopedia, 9 Dec. 2024. 
