1 NOTE {geni:about_me} '''(Curator Note:  this English Wikipedia source shows that Otto I had nine children, most other sources identify only three!  It also is inconsistent with its own external biography.  Use this source with caution!
2 CONC )'''
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Sophia of Rheineck'''
2 CONT
2 CONT Sophia of Rheineck, also known as Sophie of Salm, Countess of Bentheim (c. 1120 – 26 September 1176 in Jerusalem) was a ruling suo jure Countess of Bentheim between 1150 and 1176. She was also countess consort of Holland by marria
2 CONC ge to Dirk VI, Count of Holland, who was her co-ruler jure uxoris in the County of Bentheim.
2 CONT {{https://www.geni.com/media/proxy?media_id=6000000221713744821&size=large}}
2 CONT ''Bentheim Castle by Jacob van Ruisdael, 1653, from the National Gallery of Ireland in Dublin''
2 CONT
2 CONT Sophia was the only daughter of Otto I (appears to be inaccurate, most other sources identify Beatrix as a sister), Count of Salm, the son of the German anti-king Hermann of Salm, and Gertrude of Northeim. She was married to Dirk 
2 CONC VI, Count of Holland sometime before 1135.
2 CONT
2 CONT Sophia would fund the construction of new churches in the abbeys of Egmond and Rijnsburg. In 1138, she made a pilgrimage to Jerusalem with her husband. During their return journey, they visited the pope in Rome.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1150, Sophie inherited the County of Bentheim from her brother Otto II. Her rights were defended by her mother, who acted as her regent in Bentheim.[1] Sophie successfully claimed Bentheim, and made her spouse co-regent jure ux
2 CONC oris.
2 CONT
2 CONT After her husband's death in 1157, Sophia made a pilgrimage to Santiago de Compostella, and two more pilgrimages to Jerusalem in 1173 and 1176. During the latter visit, she died in the St. Mary's hospital of the Teutonic Knights i
2 CONC n Jerusalem.[2] She was buried in Jerusalem.[2]
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Issue?'''
2 CONT 1. Dirk, known as "the Pilgrim" (Peregrinus) (c. 1138 – 1151), buried in Egmond
2 CONT 2. Floris III (c. 1140 – 1 August 1190 at Antioch), who succeeded his father as Count of Holland in 1157
2 CONT 3. Otto (c. 1140/1145 – 1208 or later), who inherited his mother's possessions and became Count of Bentheim
2 CONT 4. Baldwin (c. 1149 – 30 April 1196), who was Provost at St. Maria in Utrecht and later Bishop of Utrecht from 1178 until his death
2 CONT 5. Dirk (c. 1152 – 28 August 1197 in Pavia), who also became Bishop of Utrecht, in 1197, but died the same year
2 CONT 6. Sophia, who became abbess of Rijnsburg Abbey in 1186
2 CONT 7. Hedwig (d. 28 August 1167), who was a nun at Rijnsburg
2 CONT 8. Gertrud, died in infancy
2 CONT 9. Petronilla
2 CONT
2 CONT Internal Sourcing:
2 CONT 1. Hildebrand, Herzog Lothar von Sachsen, p. 63.
2 CONT 2. The Teutonic Knights in the Crusader States, Indrikis Sterns, A History of the Crusades: The Impact of the Crusades on the Near East, Vol. V, ed.Norman P. Zacour and Harry W. Hazard, (University of Wisconsin Press, 1985), 319.
2 CONT Source: Wikipedia contributors. "Sophia of Rheineck." Wikipedia, The Free Encyclopedia. Wikipedia, The Free Encyclopedia, 13 Dec. 2024. Web. 13 Oct. 2025.
2 CONT …..
2 CONT '''Sophia van Rheineck'''
2 CONT Sophia van Rheineck (ca. 1120 - Jeruzalem, 26 september 1176) was getrouwd met graaf Dirk VI van Holland. Ze was de dochter van Otto van Rheineck graaf van Bentheim en Geertruid van Northeim.
2 CONT > Sophia van Rheineck (c. 1120 – Jerusalem, 26 September 1176) was married to Count Dirk VI of Holland. She was the daughter of Otto van Rheineck, Count of Bentheim, and Geertruid van Northeim.
2 CONT
2 CONT Sophia liet nieuwe abdijkerken bouwen in Egmond en Rijnsburg. In 1138 ging ze samen met haar man op pelgrimstocht naar Jeruzalem en bezocht op de terugweg de paus in Rome. Als weduwe bezocht ze Santiago de Compostella. Daarna bezo
2 CONC cht ze nog tweemaal Jeruzalem (1173) en (1176). Tijdens dat laatste bezoek overleed ze in het Mariahospitaal van de Duitse Orde in Jeruzalem. Ze werd in Jeruzalem begraven.
2 CONT > Sophia had new abbey churches built in Egmond and Rijnsburg. In 1138, she and her husband went on a pilgrimage to Jerusalem and visited the Pope in Rome on their way back. As a widow, she visited Santiago de Compostela. She subs
2 CONC equently visited Jerusalem twice more (1173) and (1176). During the latter visit, she died in the Marian Hospital of the Teutonic Order in Jerusalem. She was buried in Jerusalem.
2 CONT
2 CONT Kinderen
2 CONT '''Dirk en Sophia kregen de volgende kinderen:'''
2 CONT Dirk (ca. 1138 - 1151), begraven te Egmond
2 CONT Floris III, graaf van Holland
2 CONT Otto, graaf van Bentheim
2 CONT Boudewijn, bisschop van Utrecht
2 CONT Dirk, bisschop van Utrecht
2 CONT Sophia (ovl. na 1202), abdis van Rijnsburg
2 CONT Hadewig (ovl. Rijnsburg, 28 augustus 1167) non te Rijnsburg
2 CONT Geertruid (ovl. op 13 augustus)
2 CONT Petronilla (ovl. 5 december)
2 CONT
2 CONT Source: Bijdragers aan Wikimedia-projecten. “Sophia van Rheineck.” Wikipedia.org, Wikimedia Foundation, Inc., 14 Mar. 2007, nl.m.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sophia_van_Rheineck. Accessed 13 Oct. 2025.
2 CONT …..
2 CONT SOPHIA van RHEINECK (geb. ca. 1115 – gest. Jeruzalem 26-9-1176), door haar huwelijk gravin van Holland, op bedevaart gestorven. Dochter van Otto van Rheineck, graaf van Bentheim (ca. 1080-1150) en Geertruid van Nordheim (ca. 1091-
2 CONC 1151). Sophia van Rheineck trouwde tussen 1130 en 1135 (waarschijnlijk in 1131) met Dirk VI, graaf van Holland (ca. 1114-1157). Uit dit huwelijk werden 5 zoons en 4 dochters geboren, van wie ten minste 1 dochter jong is overleden.
2 CONT > SOPHIA van RHEINECK (b. ca. 1115 – d. Jerusalem 26-9-1176), Countess of Holland by marriage, died on a pilgrimage. Daughter of Otto van Rheineck, Count of Bentheim (c. 1080-1150) and Geertruid van Nordheim (c. 1091-1151). Sophia
2 CONC  van Rheineck married Dirk VI, Count of Holland (c. 1114-1157) between 1130 and 1135 (probably in 1131). Five sons and four daughters were born of this marriage, of whom at least one daughter died young.
2 CONT
2 CONT Sophia van Rheineck was de enige dochter (en vermoedelijk ook het enige kind) van Otto van Rheineck en Geertruid van Nordheim. Naar alle waarschijnlijkheid was het huwelijk tussen graaf Dirk en Sophia in belangrijke mate een strat
2 CONC egische alliantie: zo kon Dirk meer invloed krijgen in het Sticht tegen de bisschop van Utrecht. Sophia heeft weinig sporen nagelaten. Wel bekend is dat zij samen met haar echtgenoot optrad als patroon van de kloosters Egmond en R
2 CONC ijnsburg. Ter ere van de wijding van de nieuwe kerk van Egmond gaven Dirk en Sophia zes hoeves aan de abdij om de verlichting van de kerk te bekostigen.
2 CONT > Sophia van Rheineck was the only daughter (and likely also the only child) of Otto van Rheineck and Geertruid van Nordheim. In all likelihood, the marriage between Count Dirk and Sophia was largely a strategic alliance: this all
2 CONC owed Dirk to gain more influence in the Sticht against the Bishop of Utrecht. Sophia left few traces. What is known is that she, along with her husband, served as patron saint of the Egmond and Rijnsburg monasteries. In honor of t
2 CONC he consecration of the new church in Egmond, Dirk and Sophia donated six farms to the abbey to finance its lighting.
2 CONT
2 CONT Dat Sophia zeer vroom was, blijkt uit het feit dat zij niet minder dan drie keer de reis naar Jeruzalem maakte, en daarnaast ook nog een keer op bedevaart ging naar Santiago de Compostella in Spanje. Zeker één van de tochten naar 
2 CONC Jeruzalem maakten Sophia en haar echtgenoot samen; hun zoon Dirk werd onderweg geboren, aan welk feit hij de bijnaam ‘de pelgrim’ dankt. Op de terugweg van de reis naar Santiago werd Sophia door tussenkomst van de heilige Adalbert
2 CONC  gered toen haar gezelschap werd aangevallen door rovers. In 1173 maakte ze de reis naar Jeruzalem met een van haar zoons, Otto van Bentheim, graaf van Coevorden. Van deze reis keerde zij nooit meer terug – ze stierf in Jeruzalem 
2 CONC en werd begraven in het Diets Hospitaal aldaar. In zijn Rijmkroniek noemt Melis Stoke haar ‘heilich […] ende wijs van sinne’.
2 CONT > That Sophia was very pious is evident from the fact that she made the journey to Jerusalem no fewer than three times, and also went on a pilgrimage to Santiago de Compostela in Spain. Sophia and her husband made at least one of 
2 CONC these journeys to Jerusalem together; their son Dirk was born en route, to which he owes the nickname "the pilgrim." On the return journey from Santiago, Sophia was saved by the intervention of Saint Adalbert when her companions w
2 CONC ere attacked by robbers. In 1173, she made the journey to Jerusalem with one of her sons, Otto van Bentheim, Count of Coevorden. She never returned from this journey – she died in Jerusalem and was buried in the Diets Hospital the
2 CONC re. In his Rijmkroniek (Rhymed Chronicle), Melis Stoke calls her "holy [...] and wise in mind."
2 CONT
2 CONT Literatuur (Internal Sourcing)
