1 OCCU Er regierte die Grafschaft Stolberg und die Grafschaft Wernigerode im Harz
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} = Botho II der Ältere Graf zu Stolberg=
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Also known as Bodo the Elder'''
2 CONT
2 CONT * Son of Count Heinrich of Stolberg and Elisabeth von Mansfeld 
2 CONT * Count Bodo VII of Stolberg and Wernigerode (also known as Bodo the Elder; born: 1375; died: 15 March 1455 in Stolberg) was a German nobleman. He ruled the counties of Stolberg in the southern Harz and Wernigerode in the northern
2 CONC  Harz.
2 CONT * Noble family: House of Stolberg
2 CONT * In 1429 he became the new master of the Northern Harz Wernigerode County after the death of the last Count of Wernigerode.
2 CONT * After the death of his father, he first ruled jointly with his elder brother Henry, who died around 1416. For the first time in 1403 Botho entered independently acting in appearance was acquired as the fiefdom Rosperwenda.
2 CONT * Botho II is also known as Bodo VII. He obtains the County of Wernigerode in the northern Harz in 1429 when the last Count of Wernigerode dies. The county becomes a fief of Brandenburg again in 1449 until a partition treaty retur
2 CONC ns the county to the Stolbergs, now his great-grandson Wolfgang Graf zu Stolberg (1501). Little is known of Botho's childhood. He may have spent it at the court of Schwarzburg or Sondershausen. When his father dies, he reigns with
2 CONC  his brother Heinrich until the latter dies in 1416. Botho is not mentioned as sole ruler until 1403, when he obtains the imperial property Rosperwenda . Botho's important achievement seems to be the treaty governing the inheritan
2 CONC ce of the counties of Schwarzburg and Hohnstein (1433).
2 CONT * One of his most significant companies was the 1433 was Erbverbrüderung with the houses Schwarzburg and Hohenstein.
2 CONT * The already over 50 years old Count Botho married in June 1431, Anna, the daughter of Count Heinrich of Schwarzburg, who gave him 1433 and 1434 the son and successor of Henry and daughter Elizabeth.
2 CONT * On March 17, 1455, he was buried in the crypt at St Martin's Church in Stolberg.
2 CONT
2 CONT =Married=
2 CONT
2 CONT * Married: In June 1431, at the age of 50, Count Bodo married '''Anna, the daughter of Count Henry of Schwarzburg,''' who bore him two children: [https://fmg.ac/Projects/MedLands/THURINGIAN%20NOBILITY.htm#_Toc512066384 ANNA von Sc
2 CONC hwarzburg (-1481).  married BOTHO Graf von Stolberg-Wernigerode, son of --- (-1455)]
2 CONT
2 CONT =Children=
2 CONT
2 CONT * [https://en.m.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bodo_VII,_Count_of_Stolberg-Wernigerode '''Henry zu Stolberg,'''] born in 1433, succeeded Bodo as Count of Stolberg and Wernigerode
2 CONT * [https://en.m.wikipedia.org/wiki/William_IV,_Duke_of_Brunswick-L%C3%BCneburg '''Elizabeth zu Stolberg,'''] born in 1434, married William IV, Duke of Brunswick-Lüneburg -
2 CONT
2 CONT ==[https://en.m.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bodo_VII,_Count_of_Stolberg-Wernigerode Botho VII zu Stolberg-Wernigerode, by Wikipedia]
2 CONT
2 CONT Bodo was the son of Count Henry of Stolberg and Elizabeth née Countess of Hohenstein. Nothing is known about his youth. Perhaps he spent some time at a court of the Counts of Schwarzburg, possibly the one in the Sondershausen.
2 CONT
2 CONT After his father's death, he initially ruled jointly with his elder brother Henry, who died c. 1416. The first time Bodo is mentioned as acting alone, was when he acquired the imperial fief of Resperwenda in 1403.
2 CONT
2 CONT After the last Count of Wernigerode died in 1429, Bodo acquired the County of Wernigerode in the northern Harz.
2 CONT
2 CONT One of his most important achievements, was a treaty of inheritance with the Houses of Schwarzburg and Hohnstein in 1433.
2 CONT
2 CONT Bodo died on 15 March 1455 and was buried on 17 March in the crypt of the St. Martin's Church in Stolberg.
2 CONT
2 CONT =Links=
2 CONT
