2 CONT
2 CONT ===Biographical Accounts===
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Margaret Boyd''' was the daughter of Archibald Boyd and Christian Mure.  "Marion (''sic: see name discussion below)'' Boyd (fl. late 15th century), also known as Margot or Margaret, was a mistress of King James IV of Scotland, 
2 CONC and his first important mistress. They had three children: Alexander, born about 1490, James, who died in childhood, and Catherine, who married James Douglas, 3rd Earl of Morton."
2 CONT
2 CONT She was related to Thomas Boyd, Earl of Arran, and a niece of the second wife of Archibald Douglas, 5th Earl of Angus. Her relationship with the king was linked with the rise and fall of Angus's influence at the court.
2 CONT
2 CONT >"Archibald’s sister-in-law, '''Margaret Boyd''', his wife’s second younger sister, was in her youth mistress to King James IV, and bore him Alexander Stewart, Archbishop of St Andrews, and Jean (''sic: Catherine'') countess of Mo
2 CONC rton. Margaret thus found herself much in favour in court, and in addition she was cousin to Dame Elisabeth Boyd daughter of Thomas Lord Boyd, and to Countess Of Angus, whose husband Archibald, Earl of Angus, (then Chamberlain) ha
2 CONC d the greatest power in the state."
2 CONT
2 CONT ==Family==
2 CONT
2 CONT Children of Margaret Boyd and James IV Stewart, King of Scotland
2 CONT # Catherine Stewart+ d. a 1554 
2 CONT # Alexander Stewart b. c 1493, d. 1513
2 CONT
2 CONT Children of Margaret Boyd and John Mure
2 CONT # Mungo Mure,  b. Abt 1500, of Rowallan, Kilmarnock, Ayr, Scotland ,  d. 10 Sep 1547, Battlefield, Pinkie, Inveresk, Midlothian, Scotland
2 CONT # Alexander Mure,  b. Abt 1509, of, Ormshugh, Ayr, Scotland , 
2 CONT # Patrick Mure,  b. Abt 1511, of Rowallan, Kilmarnock, Ayr, Scotland , 
2 CONT # Adam Mure,  b. Abt 1513, of Rowallan, Kilmarnock, Ayr, Scotland , 
2 CONT # Elizabeth Mure,  b. Abt 1515, of Rowallan, Kilmarnock, Ayr, Scotland , 
2 CONT # Marion Mure,  b. Abt 1517, of Rowallan, Kilmarnock, Ayr, Scotland , 
2 CONT # Margaret Mure,  b. Abt 1519, of Rowallan, Kilmarnock, Ayr, Scotland , 
2 CONT # Marjory Mure,  b. Abt 1520, of Rowallan, Kilmarnock, Ayr, Scotland , 
2 CONT
2 CONT Dunbar’s paternal grandmother was Janet Mure of Rowallan, whose parents were John Mure of Rowallan, killed at Flodden, and Margaret Boyd, the mistress of James IV. As the daughter of Archibald Boyd of Bonshaw, Margaret Boyd was a 
2 CONC grand-daughter of Robert, first Lord Boyd [d. 1482]. See Stirnet mureO1 genealogy. His Boyd descent clearly meant quite a lot to Dunbar, as well it might. They were a major noble family, not least in the southwest, and the Mures o
2 CONC f Rowallan remained closely linked to them. If Dunbar is correct about Janet’s first husband, he was a Maxwell of Newark (a castle on the Clyde in what is now Port Glasgow). This is certainly claimed by the poet Sir William Mure o
2 CONC f Rowallan on pp.75f. of The Historie and Descent of the House of Rowallane (Glasgow, 1825), who says her second husband was John Lockhart [d.1528] of Bar (in Galston, Ayrshire). Jonet’s younger sister Alison married this man’s so
2 CONC n — also called John — who was a fierce Protestant, and who invited John Knox to preach in Barr Castle in 1556 (Knox, History of the Reformation I.250). 
2 CONT
2 CONT == MARION or MARGARET? ==
2 CONT
2 CONT Douglas Richardson discusses her name here:
2 CONT
2 CONT http://archiver.rootsweb.ancestry.com/th/read/GEN-MEDIEVAL/2013-10/...
2 CONT
2 CONT "As to Margaret Boyd being called Marion Boyd in secondary sources, that error seems to have arisen from an unfortunate mistake by George Burnett in Exchequer Rolls of Scotland 12 (1889): xl–xli, in which he naively assumed that M
2 CONC argaret Boyd, the mistress of King James IV, was the same person as a Marion Boyd mentioned in passing in the Exchequer rolls. It apparently didn't trouble him that the given names Margaret and Marion were not interchangeable in t
2 CONC his period."
2 CONT
2 CONT "Burnett's misidentification of Margaret Boyd as being the same person as Marion Boyd was subsequently repeated by Herkless & Hannay, Archbishops of St. Andrews 1 (1907): 219. The error has continued to have life until today. But 
2 CONC it was elsewhere rejected by two reliable standard Scottish sources, namely Scots Peerage 1 (1904): 22; 5 (1908): 145–146; 6 (1909): 361 and by Dunbar, Scottish Kings (1906): 220."
2 CONT
2 CONT Citations
