1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Margareta Tudor, born November 28, 1489 in the Palace of Westminster in London, died October 18, 1541, was an English princess, Queen of Scotland, and later Regent of Scotland during the incapacity of her son Jacob
2 CONC  in 1513–1514. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Her marriage to King James IV of Scotland was intended to bring peace between the two countries of Scotland and England, Which had long been at feud. However, this failed and the king was killed at the Battle of Flodden. Margaret 
2 CONC then caused scandal by remarrying the Earl of Angus, against her brother's wishes, and then seeking a divorce from him.
2 CONT
2 CONT Margaret's great-grandson, James, became the king who united Scotland and England, despite Margaret and her descendants being Excluded from the English line of succession by Margaret's brother, Henry VIII of England.
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT https://thefreelancehistorywriter.com/2021/10/15/margaret-tudors-progress-to-scotland-to-marry-james-iv/
2 CONT Margaret Tudor 
2 CONT On Oct 18th 1541 Margaret Tudor died at Methven Castle in Scotland, and though history often overlooks this daughter of Henry VII and sister of Henry VIII, she is perhaps the most genealogically significant Tudor who ever lived – 
2 CONC the woman whose bloodline became the bridge connecting every British Monarch since 1707 to over 1,000 years of English royal ancestry stretching back to the Anglo Saxon kings who ruled before William the conqueror ever set foot on
2 CONC
2 CONT English shores. 
2 CONT Born in 1489 as the eldest daughter of Henry VII and Elizabeth of York, Margaret carried within her veins the merged bloodlines of the Houses of York and Lancaster, ending the Wars of the Roses, but through her mother Elizabeth, s
2 CONC he also inherited something even more precious, the ancient blood of the House of Wessex, the dynasty of Alfred the Great and Athelstan, England’s first true king, a lineage that had survived the Norman Conquest and flowed through
2 CONC  generations of Plantagenet, Angevin and Tudor monarchs.
2 CONT In 1503, at just 13 years old, Margaret made the perilous journey north to marry James IV of Scotland in a union designed to bring peace between the perpetually warring nations, and though she endured heartbreak when James Died at
2 CONC  the Battle of Flodden in 1513 and only one of her 6 children survived infancy – James V of Scotland – that single surviving son carried forward the precious bloodline that would change everything.
2 CONT Margaret’s great grandson was James VI of Scotland, who became James I of England in 1603, uniting the crowns and when the form, every single British monarch formal Act of Union in 1707, every single British Monarch from that mome
2 CONC nt on descended directly from Margaret Tudor, making her the genealogical mother of the modern monarchy
2 CONT
2 CONT Today, King Charles III is her 14x great grandson, carrying within him the blood of Alfred the Great, William the Conqueror, the Plantagenets and the Tudors, all because one Tudor princess travelled north, married a Scottish king 
2 CONC and became the living link that connected a millennium of English royal history to every monarch who would follow, proving that sometimes the most important figures in history aren’t the ones who wore crowns themselves, but the on
2 CONC es whose bloodlines ensured crowns would continue.
2 CONT www.oldhistoryreflections/margaret tudor, 5 Jan 2026
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT
