0 @N6000000002187780999@ NOTE Frederick I of Denmark
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Frederick I
1 CONT King of Denmark and Norway
1 CONT King Frederick I
1 CONT Born October 7, 1471
1 CONT Died April 10, 1533
1 CONT Buried Roskilde Cathedral
1 CONT Predecessor Christian II
1 CONT Successor Christian III
1 CONT Consort Anna of Brandenburg
1 CONT Sophie of Pomerania
1 CONT Father Christian I
1 CONT Mother Dorothea of Brandenburg
1 CONT
1 CONT Frederick I of Denmark and Norway (October 7, 1471 – April 10, 1533) was the son of the first Oldenburg King Christian I of Denmark, Norway and Sweden (1426–1481) and of Dorothea of Brandenburg (1430–1495). The name is also spelle
1 CONC d Friedrich in German, Frederik in Danish, and Fredrik in Swedish and Norwegian.
1 CONT
1 CONT The underage Frederick was elected co-Duke of Schleswig and Holstein in 1482, soon after the death of his father, the other co-duke being his ten years elder brother Johann (or Hans), the King. At Frederick's majority in 1490 both
1 CONC  duchies were divided between the brothers.
1 CONT
1 CONT In 1500 he'd convinced his brother and co-duke for a conquest of Dithmarschen, and a great army was called from not only the duchies, but with additions from all of the Kalmar Union for which his brother briefly was king. Also num
1 CONC erous German mercenaries took part. The expedition failed however miserably in the Battle of Hemmingstedt, where one third of all knights of Schleswig and Holstein lost their lives.
1 CONT
1 CONT In 1523 his nephew Christian II, the King of Denmark and Sweden, was forced by disloyal nobles to abdicate, and the duke took the throne as king Frederick I. A group of Jutish nobles had offered Frederick the throne as early as 15
1 CONC 13, when his brother king Hans died, but he had declined, rightly believing that the majority of the Danish nobility would be loyal to prince Christian.
1 CONT
1 CONT During his rule as a Danish king Frederick had to suppress social revolts among the peasants at the same time as the rise of the Protestant movement made a balancing attitude a necessity. Without being a man of greater statesmansh
1 CONC ip Frederick managed to escape all open conflicts even though he seems to have accepted the spread of Lutheran propaganda. 1532 he succeeded in capturing Christian II who had tried to get a political come-back in Norway.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Family and children
1 CONT
1 CONT In 1502 he married Anna of Brandenburg (15 years old; a daughter of the cousin of his mother; 1487–1514). The couple had two children:
1 CONT
1 CONT    1. Christian, the future duke and king (12 August 1503 – 1 January 1559)
1 CONT    2. Dorothea (1 August 1504 – 11 April 1547), married 1 July 1526 to Duke Albert of Prussia.
1 CONT
1 CONT Frederick's wife Anna died on May 5, 1514, 26 years old.
1 CONT
1 CONT Four years later, Frederick married Sophie of Pomerania (20 years old; 1498–1568), a daughter of Duke Bogislaw "the Great" of Pomerania. Sophie and Frederick had six children:
1 CONT
1 CONT    1. Duke John of Holstein (28 June 1521 – 2 October 1580)
1 CONT    2. Elizabeth (14 October 1524 – 15 October 1586), married:
1 CONT          1. on 26 August 1543 to Duke Magnus III of Mecklenburg-Schwerin
1 CONT          2. on 14 February 1556 to Duke Ulrich III of Mecklenburg-Güstrow
1 CONT    3. Duke Adolf of Holstein-Gottorp (25 January 1526 – 1 October 1586)
1 CONT    4. Anna (1527 – 4 June 1535)
1 CONT    5. Dorothea (1528 – 11 November 1575), married on 27 October 1573 to Duke Christof of Mecklenburg-Schwerin.
1 CONT    6. Bishop Friedrich of Hildesheim and Schleswig (13 April 1532 – 27 October 1556).
1 CONT
1 CONT In 1525 his son, the future king Christian III, married Dorothea of Saxe-Lauenburg (14 years old; 1511–1571).
1 CONT
1 CONT He died on April 10, 1533 in Gottorp, at an age of 61.
1 CONT Preceded by
1 CONT Christian II King of Denmark
1 CONT 1523–1533 Succeeded by
1 CONT Christian III
1 CONT King of Norway
1 CONT 1523–1533
