0 @N6000000004533048427@ NOTE Sophie, Grevinde af Orlamiinde, -1181-, ældste Datter af
1 CONT Kong Valdemar og Dronning Sophie; hun blev i Slesvig bortgiftet
1 CONT til Grev Sigfred af Orlamiinde i Thiiringen (1181). Han sendtes
1 CONT et Par Aar senere af Kejser Frederik til Kong Knud med
1 CONT P'or-langende om, at denne skulde hylde Kejseren som Lensherre, men
1 CONT blev djærvt afvist. Om S.s Skæbne vides i øvrigt intet; hun fødte
1 CONT sin Ægtefælle 7 Børn, af hvilke Albert, hvis Historie tilhører
1 CONT Danmark (I, 168), var den ældste. Sigfred døde 1206.
1 CONT
1 CONT Johannes C. H. R. Steenstrup.
0 @N6000000004533196561@ NOTE <b>Albert,</b> Greve af Orlamünde, 1182-1245. Hans Forældre vare GrevSigfred af Orlamünde og Sophie, Kong Valdemar I's ældste Datter. Dehavde holdt Bryllup i Efteraaret 1181, og Aaret efter fødtes A., denældste af 7 Søskende. 1204
1 CONC  fik han af sin Morbroder, Kong Valdemar II,Grevskabet Holsten til Len tillige med en Del af Grevskabet Ratzeburg,
1 CONT hvortil senere ved Faderens Død (1206) kom et Stykke af GrevskabetOrlamünde. Dette sit Hjemlands Styrelse har A. imidlertid i detvæsentlige maattet overlade til sin Broder Herman, hvem det øvrige afOrlamünde tilfaldt, da han selv 
1 CONC sin meste Tid har maattet opholde sigi sit Len eller dog været virksom i dettes Anliggender.
1 CONT
1 CONT I de første Aar af Valdemar II's Regering var der jævnlig Ufred paaDanmarks Sydgrænse, snart var det Valdemar, som angreb sine sydligeNaboer, snart var det disse, der gjorde Gjengjæld, og A. kunde somLensmand i Rigets sydligste Pr
1 CONC ovins ikke undgaa at tage Del i disseFejder. I Aaret 1215 (der krigedes i dette og det følgende Aar mod etForbund af nordtyske Fyrster, som understøttedes af den tyske Kejser)gjorde A. et helligt Løfte om at ville drage til Liflan
1 CONC d for at kæmpemod Hedningene der. Han fik pavelig Tilladelse til at modtage KorsetsTegn, opnaaede desuden kort efter som en særlig Begunstigelse afKirkens Overhoved at maatte tage 10 af sine Mænd, der tidligere havdeforbundet sig 
1 CONC til
1 CONT et Pilegrimstog til det hellige Land, med sig, og saa snart der varindtraadt Fred, indskibede han sig i Sommeren 1217 med sit Følge tilLifland. Hans Færd paa dette Tog berømmes i Henrik LettesLiflandskrønike i følgende Ord: «Han v
1 CONC ar en udvalgt Pil, den Herrenhavde gjemt i sit Kogger for i et heldigt Øjeblik at sende ham tilLifland til Kirkens Frelse.» Det
1 CONT følgende Aar vendte han tilbage over Danmark og var uden Tvivltilstede ved den unge Kong Valdemar III's Kroning i Slesvig (24. Juni1218). Et Par Aars Tid synes han nu at have været i Ro, saa komAngrebet paa Greven af Schwerins Lan
1 CONC de, som A. tog i Besiddelse paasin Morbroders Vegne, hvorefter fulgte Ulykkesaaret 1223, da KongValdemar og hans ældste Søn kom i Henrik af Schwerins Vold. Under dederved bevirkede Forhold kom A., Valdemars nære Slægtning og betro
1 CONC edeMand, til at øve en afgjørende Indflydelse paa mange af DanmarksAnliggender, og om han end ikke havde Navn af Rigsforstander, saaoptraadte han dog i den følgende Tid nogle Gange med enRigsforstanders Myndighed. Saaledes under F
1 CONC orhandlingerne om KongensLøsladelse (1224), i Anledning af hvilke han tvende Gange var iTyskland, første Gang da han deltog i Afslutningen af det saakaldtedannenbergske Forlig, og anden Gang da dette Forlig skulde fuldbyrdes,og A.
1 CONC  og de danske Stormænd vare mødte med den betingede Løsesum, menmaatte vende hjem med
1 CONT uforrettet Sag. A. havde tidligere (i Aaret 1211) stiftet Klosteret iPreetz, nu efter Hjemkomsten fra Tyskland grundlagde han (Nov. 1224)et andet Kloster i Hoibek, der i øvrigt senere forlagdes til Reinbek;men snart bleve hans Tan
1 CONC ker dragne andensteds hen. Ved Juletid 1224faldt nemlig Grev Adolf af Schaumburg, der nylig havde sluttet sigsammen med Ærkebispen af Bremen og Grev Henrik af Schwerin om atløsrive Holsten fra Danmark, med sine medforbundne ind i 
1 CONC Holsten. A.samlede sin Krigsmagt, og efter at have søgt at knytte Borgerne iHamborg til sig ved at tilstaa dem forskjellige Begunstigelser mødtehan i Forening med sin Fætter, Hertug Otto af Brunsvig, de forbundneved Mølln i Slutni
1 CONC ngen af Jan. 1225. Efter en hel Dags tapper Modstandblev A. her overvunden og tagen til Fange af Grev Henrik. Først efterNederlaget ved Bornhøvede, og efter at A. havde overgivet BorgenLauenborg, der endnu holdtes besat af hans Fo
1 CONC lk, fik han igjen sinFrihed (1227), men Holsten var tabt for stedse. A. tog nu for en kortTid Ophold i sine thiiringske Besiddelser og vendte derpaa tilbage tilDanmark. Her fik han af Kong Valdemar dennes Gods paa Als i Forlening-
1 CONC - han kalder sig 1229 «Herre til Als» -- og nævnes derefter kun noglefaa Gange, sidste Gang 1238, inden sin Død. Han døde 1245; Dødsdagenkjendes ikke, men maa ligge forud for 22. Okt., paa hvilken Dag KongValdemars Sønner netop i 
1 CONC Anledning af A.'s Død skiftede Godset paa Alsimellem sig. A. havde i Aaret 1211 ægtet Landgreve Herman afThüringens Datter Hedevig, men havde ingen Børn i sit Ægteskab.
1 CONT
1 CONT R. Usinger, Deutsch-dänische Geschichte 1189-1227.
1 CONT C. Paludan-Müller, Studier til Danmarks Hist. i 13. Aarh., 1. Stykke.
0 @N6000000000337169788@ NOTE (Research):Ottocar I Ottocar I Pronounced As: otkär or Premysl Ottocar I przhemisl ôtôkär , d. 1230, duke (1197-98) and king (1198-1230) of Bohemia. The struggle within the Holy Roman Empire for the imperial crown enabled Ottocar 
1 CONC to obtain (1198) from Philip of Swabia the royal title. Holy Roman Emperor Frederick II confirmed the title (1212) and the election (1216) of Ottocar's son (later Wenceslaus I) as his successor. In 1221, Ottocar was forced to surr
1 CONC ender disciplinary rights over the clergy to the bishop of Prague but retained control over secular church affairs. ======================================================== Przemysl Ottokar I, King of Bohemia 1198-1230 (1155-1230)
1 CONC   Born circa 1155   Died 15 December 1230  Married circa 1198 Konstanze of Hungary  Died 4 December 1240   Initially confirmed as Duke of Bohemia in 1192 by Emperor Heinrich VI, he was deposed the following year but regained posse
1 CONC ssion of Bohemia in 1197. In 1198 he was acknowledged as King of Bohemia by Emperor Philipp and had almost total autonomy for Bohemia. His title was later  confirmed by Emperor Friedrich II in 1212 and this extinguished the contro
1 CONC l of the Empire over Bohemian affairs. Przemysl Ottokar I then established a basis for a strong Bohemian state and the hereditary rights of his family. Source: Leo van de Pas
0 @N6000000002187828312@ NOTE Ottokar I of Bohemia
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Otakar I (also spelled Ottokar or Premysl I. Otakar) (c. 1155 – December 15, 1230), king of Bohemia (1198 - 1230), was a younger son of King Vladislav II (d. 1174) and a member of the Premyslid dynasty.
1 CONT
1 CONT His early years were passed amid the anarchy which prevailed everywhere in the country, after several struggles, in which he took part, he was recognized as ruler of Bohemia by the emperor Henry VI in 1192. He was, however, soon o
1 CONC verthrown for joining a conspiracy of German princes to bring down the Hohenstaufen monarchy. In 1197 Otakar forced his brother, duke Vladislav Jindrich, to abandon Bohemia to him and to content himself with Moravia. Taking advant
1 CONC age of the German civil war between the Hohenstaufen claimant Philip of Swabia and the Welf candidate Otto IV, Ottokar declared himself King of Bohemia. This title was supported by Philip of Swabia who needed Czech military suppor
1 CONC t against Otto (1198).
1 CONT
1 CONT In 1199 he divorced his wife, Adelheid of Meissen, a member of the Wettin (dynasty) for a marriage with Constance of Hungary, the young daughter of the Hungarian King Bela III.
1 CONT
1 CONT In 1200 - with Otto IV in the ascendancy, Otakar abandoned his pact with Philip and declared for the Welf faction. Both Otto and Pope Innocent III subsequently accepted Otakar as hereditary King of Bohemia.
1 CONT
1 CONT Philip's consequent invasion of Bohemia was successful. Otakar, having been compelled to pay a fine, again ranged himself among Philip's partisans and still later was among the supporters of the young king, Frederick II. In 1212 F
1 CONC rederick granted the Golden Bull of Sicily to Bohemia. This document recognised Otakar and his heirs as Kings of Bohemia. The King was no longer subject to appointment by the Emperor, and was only required to attend Diets close to
1 CONC  the Bohemian border. Although a subject of the Holy Roman Empire, the Bohemian King was to be the leading electoral prince of the empire and to furnish all subsequent Emperors with a bodyguard of 300 knights when they went to Rom
1 CONC e for their coronation.
1 CONT
1 CONT Otakar's reign was also notable for the start of German immigration into Bohemia and the growth of towns in what had until that point been forest lands. In 1226 Otakar went to war against Frederick II of Austria after the latter w
1 CONC recked a deal which would have seen Otakar's daughter (Saint Agnes of Bohemia) married to Emperor Frederick II's son, Henry II of Sicily. Otakar then planned for the same daughter to marry Henry III of England, but this was vetoed
1 CONC  by the Emperor who knew Henry to be an opponent of the Hohenstaufen dynasty. Then widowed Emperor himself wanted to marry Agnes, but by then she didn't want to play a role in an arranged marriage anymore. With the help of the pop
1 CONC e, Agnes entered the convent.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Family
1 CONT
1 CONT Ottokar was married first in 1178 to Adelheid of Meissen (after 1160 - February 2, 1211), who gave birth to the following children:
1 CONT
1 CONT     * Vratislav (before 1181 - after 1225)
1 CONT     * Dagmar (Markéta) (ca 1186 - May 24, 1213), married to Valdemar II of Denmark, King of Denmark
1 CONT     * Božislava
1 CONT     * Hedvika
1 CONT
1 CONT In 1199, he married Constance of Hungary (1181 – December 6, 1240), who gave birth to the following children:
1 CONT
1 CONT     * Vratislav (ca 1200 – ?)
1 CONT     * Judith (- June 2, 1230), married to Bernard of Carinthia, Duke of Carinthia
1 CONT     * Anne (Anna Lehnická) (1204 - June 23, 1265), married to Henry II the Pious, Duke of Wroclaw
1 CONT     * Anežka
1 CONT     * Wenceslaus I of Bohemia (ca 1205 - September 23, 1253), King of Bohemia
1 CONT     * Vladislaus (Vratislav) (1207 - February 18, 1227), Margrave of Moravia
1 CONT     * Premyslid (Premysl) (1209 - October 16, 1239), Margrave of Moravia, married to Margaret of Meran
1 CONT     * Wilhelmina of Bohemia (Vilemína Ceská, Guglielmina Boema) (1210 - October 24, 1281)
1 CONT     * Saint Agnes of Bohemia (January 20, 1211 – March 6, 1282)
1 CONT
1 CONT
