0 @N6000000002187868927@ NOTE James V of Scotland
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT James V (April 10, 1512 – December 14, 1542) was King of Scotland (September 9, 1513 – December 14, 1542).
1 CONT
1 CONT The son of King James IV of Scotland, he was born in April 10, 11 or 15, 1512, at Linlithgow Palace, West Lothian, and was still an infant when his father was killed at the Battle of Flodden Field on September 9, 1513.
1 CONT
1 CONT He was crowned in the Chapel Royal at Stirling Castle on September 21, 1513. During his childhood, the country was ruled by regents, first by his mother, Margaret Tudor (sister of King Henry VIII of England), until she remarried i
1 CONC n the following year, and thereafter by John, Duke of Albany, who was himself next in line for the throne after James and his younger brother, the posthumously-born Alexander, Duke of Ross. However, when war broke out again betwee
1 CONC n England and France, the 6th Earl of Angus, the young king's stepfather, drove out Albany and kept James confined at Edinburgh Castle. Margaret, having divorced Angus, rescued James, and in 1528 he assumed the reins of government
1 CONC .
1 CONT
1 CONT His first action as king was to remove Angus from the scene, and he then subdued the Border rebels and the chiefs of the Western Isles. James V increased his royal income by tightening control over the royal estates and from the p
1 CONC rofits of justice, customs and feudal rights. He also gave his illegitimate sons lucrative benefices, thereby diverting substantial church wealth into his coffers. James spent a large amount of his wealth on building work at Stirl
1 CONC ing, Falkland, Linlithgow and Holyrood.
1 CONT
1 CONT James renewed the Auld Alliance with France, and on January 1, 1537, he married Madeleine de Valois, the daughter of King Francis I of France. Following her death a few months later, he proceeded to marry Marie of Guise, the daugh
1 CONC ter of Claude, 1st Duke of Guise and the widow of Louis of Orleans, Duke of Longueville. Although Mary already had two children from her first marriage, both her sons by James died in infancy.
1 CONT
1 CONT King James V did not tolerate heresy, and during his reign a number of outspoken supporters of church reform were executed. The most famous of the reformers sentenced to death was Patrick Hamilton who was burned at the stake as a 
1 CONC heretic at St Andrews in 1528.
1 CONT
1 CONT He had two legitimate sons by Marie of Guise, James Stewart, Duke of Rothesay (b. May 22, 1540), and Arthur Stewart, Duke of Albany (b. 1541). However, both died in infancy in April 1541, the second just eight days after his bapti
1 CONC sm.
1 CONT
1 CONT The death of his mother in 1541 removed any incentive for keeping peace with England, and James was defeated at the Battle of Solway Moss in 1542. The setback affected his health, which had been poor for some time, and he was on h
1 CONC is deathbed at Falkland Palace on December 8 when his only living heir, a girl, was born. Before he died, he is reported to have said, "It began with a lass and it will end with a lass". This was a reference to the Stewart dynasty
1 CONC , and how it had come to the throne through Marjorie, the daughter of Robert the Bruce. As it happened, the House of Stewart retained the throne despite its passing through an heiress, due to Mary's marriage to Lord Darnley, a dis
1 CONC tant Stewart cousin.
1 CONT
1 CONT James was succeeded by his infant daughter, Mary, Queen of Scots. He was buried at Holyrood Abbey alongside Madeleine and his sons by Marie de Guise.
1 CONT
1 CONT James V fathered seven known illegitimate children, three before the age of twenty. James Stewart, 1st Earl of Moray, his son by his favourite mistress, went on to play an important part in the reigns of Mary, Queen of Scots and J
1 CONC ames VI.
1 CONT
1 CONT James would disguise himself and walk about in Edinbugh at night, using the made-up name Goodman of Ballengeich.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT References
1 CONT Donaldson, Gordon (1965). Scotland: James V to James VII. Edinburgh: Oliver & Boyd.
1 CONT
1 CONT Preceded by:
1 CONT James IV King of Scots
1 CONT 1513–1542 Succeeded by:
1 CONT Mary I 
0 @N6000000002322323833@ NOTE James V died 6 days after the birth of his daughter and heiress to the throne
1 CONT of Scotland, Mary Queen of Scots. He reigned as King from 1513-1542.
